<<~ ahu #/head>>

! quorum-entry ~ one byte-image, two boards, no drift

The Kapae antigen board (`kapae-antigen`) and the members set (`membership-registry`) stand the SAME structure:
a monotone per-nym CRDT of quorum-signed steward acts. Their signed bytes carry the identical five fields in
the identical order — only the `kind` DOMAIN separates them. This module holds that common image once, so the
two boards can never drift a field apart while both claiming a quorum verified them.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-subtraction>>

!! The subtraction ~ the shared act beneath two boards

`QuorumEntryFields` names the steward act every quorum board carries: `kind` (the signing domain), `nym` (the
subject's verifying-key hex — an identity, never a doc), `action` (the board-local act — `kapae`/`un_kapae`,
`admit`/`revoke`), `version` (monotone per nym; a higher verified version supersedes a lower), and
`sealEpochCid` (the charter epoch the act roots on — an entry citing an unknown epoch is IGNORED).

`quorumEntryBytes` canonicalises exactly those five, sorted-key stable. ''The signatures ride OUTSIDE the
signed content'', so re-carrying an entry never re-signs it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/domain-first>>

!! The domain stays first and stays required ~ the cross-board separation

`kind` rides as the FIRST canonical field and every caller supplies its own domain constant — ''that value IS
the cross-board separation.'' An antigen signature and a membership signature cover different bytes because
they cover different domains, so no signature raised on one board can ever be re-presented on the other. A
shared image with an optional or defaulted domain would dissolve exactly that guarantee; this signature keeps
the domain un-defaultable.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/refuses-a-verdict>>

!! What this module refuses ~ bytes only, never a verdict

The module carries the BYTES ONLY. The two boards gate DIFFERENTLY on purpose: the members set additionally
requires the operator's OWN accepts-carriage contract token (`carriageContractBytes`), and the antigen
requires no such consent. ''A shared verifier that forgot that token would turn admission into conscription.''
So the fold and the quorum verdicts stay board-local, deliberately — this module names the common bytes and
declines every judgment about them.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-one-door>>

!! The one door ~ every Ed25519 signature and every key reads one strict rule

Every verifier in the house calls `verifyEd25519`, and every place a public key enters standing state calls
`acceptEd25519Key`. Both live in `ed25519-door.ts`, and no other source file calls a curve library's
`verify`: a census test reads the whole source tree and turns red on the first one that does.

The rule takes no option:

* a key reads as 32 bytes, in its ONE canonical encoding, on the curve, and INSIDE the prime-order subgroup.
  A small-order key holds no secret, so under a lenient rule ''everyone signs for it''; the door refuses it
  by name, `key-small-order`. A mixed-order key (a prime-order point plus a torsion point) refuses
  `key-mixed-order`: no honest key generation yields one, and it stands as the one key whose signatures two
  lawful verifiers may read differently. The check costs one scalar multiplication per key.
* a signature reads as 64 bytes `R ‖ S`, with `S` below the group order and `R` canonical and on the curve.
  ''A small-order `R` refuses'' (`signature-point-small-order`), as libsodium and dalek's strict verify
  refuse it, though RFC 8032 alone would let it pass.
* the equation reads COFACTORLESS, the sufficient form RFC 8032 §5.1.7 states: `[S]B = R + [k]A`. No factor
  of eight forgives a torsion component.
* ''hex reads bare and lower-case alone.'' One key holds one spelling wherever a seat set or a signer dedup
  compares strings; an upper-case or `0x`-prefixed copy of a key names nobody at the door.

''A key refuses where it ENTERS, by name'': a charter seat, a roll's seated and revealed sets, a recovery contact
registration, a persona inception's op-key and each rotation's fresh one, a Handle key, both keys a device
edge names, the device key an admit names. A charter, a roll or a log that names a refused key refuses WHOLE.
Dropping the one seat would move a threshold nobody signed for.

''A weak key hidden inside a commitment refuses at its reveal, and the charter or face that committed it
re-founds'': the reveal refuses whole, no roll or rotation can ever name that set lawfully, and nothing
rescues the seat by counting it for nothing. So a committer checks before it hides: `seatNextDigest`, the
recovery contact registration card and each minter pass the key through the door before any digest covers it.

No verifier takes its verify as a parameter where it can call the door itself: a function handed its verifier
verifies as leniently as its caller chose.

The door changes which signatures and keys get ACCEPTED. It moves no signing domain and no signed byte: every
producer the house uses (noble, Node `crypto`, WebCrypto with a non-extractable key) signs by RFC 8032 and
passes.

<<~/ahu>>

